# Map Guide 2 · Part A India, chapters 4 to 6.
P = lambda n, g, x, **kw: dict(n=n, g=g, x=x, **kw)

CH_INDIA_B = [
dict(id="coasts", part="India", t="Coasts, islands and lakes", intro=(
 "India's coastline is about 7,500 km long (about 11,000 km in the 2023–24 re-measurement, which counted every bend). The west coast is narrow and cut by short, fast rivers; its south part, the Malabar coast of Kerala, has backwaters and lagoons. "
 "The east coast (Coromandel in the south, the Northern Circars in the north) is wide and flat, with big deltas and lagoons such as Chilika and Pulicat. A lagoon is a stretch of salty or brackish water (part sea, part fresh) cut off from the sea by a sandbar. "
 "Off the coasts lie two island groups: Lakshadweep, low coral islands in the Arabian Sea, and the Andaman and Nicobar Islands, the tops of a drowned mountain chain in the Bay of Bengal. "
 "Lake questions usually ask for a superlative: the largest, the longest, the saltiest. Pin each superlative to its lake."), P=[
 P("Rann of Kutch", [70.00, 23.90], "A huge salt marsh in Kutch, Gujarat. In the monsoon the sea and rivers flood it; in winter it dries into a flat white sheet of salt (the Rann Utsav is held then). The Great Rann lies to the north, on the Pakistan border; the Little Rann to the south-east is home to the Indian wild ass. Sir Creek, a disputed strip of water with Pakistan, is at its western end. Dholavira, a big Harappan city and World Heritage Site, is on an island in the Rann."),
 P("Gulf of Khambhat", [72.40, 21.60], "Also called the Gulf of Cambay, an inlet of the Arabian Sea between the Kathiawar peninsula and mainland Gujarat. The Narmada, Tapi, Mahi and Sabarmati all end here. Its tides are among the highest on India's coast, so it is studied for tidal power. Lothal, the Harappan dockyard town, stood near its head; Alang, the world's largest ship-breaking yard, is on its west shore."),
 P("Gulf of Kutch", [69.60, 22.60], "An inlet between Kutch and the Kathiawar peninsula, Gujarat. India's first Marine National Park (1980–82) protects its coral reefs and mangroves. The big ports of Kandla (Deendayal Port) and Mundra are on it. Like Khambhat, it has strong tides suited to tidal energy."),
 P("Palk Strait", [79.50, 10.00], "The strip of sea between Tamil Nadu and the Jaffna peninsula of Sri Lanka, named after Robert Palk, a Madras governor. It joins the Bay of Bengal (through Palk Bay) to the Gulf of Mannar. It is shallow, so big ships go round Sri Lanka. Fishing rights here and the island of Katchatheevu, given to Sri Lanka in 1974, are an India–Sri Lanka issue."),
 P("Adam's Bridge (Ram Setu)", [79.50, 9.12], "A chain of sandbanks and shoals about 48 km long from Pamban Island (Rameswaram) to Mannar Island in Sri Lanka. In the Ramayana it is the bridge built by Rama's army. It divides the Palk Strait (north) from the Gulf of Mannar (south). The Sethusamudram shipping canal plan to cut through it has been stopped for years."),
 P("Gulf of Mannar", [79.10, 8.90], "The sea south of Rameswaram, between Tamil Nadu and Sri Lanka. Its 21 small islands and coral reefs form the Gulf of Mannar Marine National Park (1986), and the wider area was India's first marine Biosphere Reserve (1989). It is home to the dugong, or sea cow, a plant-eating sea mammal now rare; a dugong conservation reserve was set up in Palk Bay in 2022. It is also famous for pearls and chanks (conch shells)."),
 P("Kanyakumari", [77.54, 8.08], "Cape Comorin, the southern tip of mainland India, in Tamil Nadu, where the Arabian Sea, the Indian Ocean and the Bay of Bengal meet. The Vivekananda Rock Memorial (where Swami Vivekananda meditated in 1892) and the 133-foot Thiruvalluvar statue stand on rocks offshore; a glass bridge joined them in 2024. The southernmost point of India as a whole is Indira Point in the Nicobars."),
 P("Vembanad Lake", [76.38, 9.60], "The longest lake in India (about 96 km), a backwater lagoon in Kerala stretching past Alappuzha (Alleppey) and Kochi. It is a Ramsar site (a wetland of international importance under the Ramsar Convention of 1971). The Nehru Trophy snake-boat race is held on its Punnamada stretch every August. Willingdon Island, Kochi port, is in it."),
 P("Pulicat Lake", [80.30, 13.55], "The second largest brackish-water lagoon in India after Chilika, on the Andhra Pradesh–Tamil Nadu border, north of Chennai. The long barrier island of Sriharikota, where ISRO launches rockets, separates it from the Bay of Bengal. Brackish means a mix of salt sea water and fresh river water. It has bird sanctuaries on both sides of the border and is famous for flamingos in winter. The Dutch had a fort at Pulicat in the 1600s."),
 P("Chilika Lake", [85.33, 19.70], "The largest brackish-water lagoon in India and the second largest in the world, in Odisha (Puri, Khurda, Ganjam). It was India's first Ramsar site in 1981 (with Keoladeo). Nalabana island in it is a bird sanctuary for winter visitors from Siberia, and the Satapada area has Irrawaddy dolphins. A new mouth cut in 2000 to the sea revived it, and it was taken off the Montreux Record (the list of Ramsar sites in danger) in 2002.", s="Odisha"),
 P("Sambhar Lake", [75.08, 26.92], "The largest inland salt lake in India, near Jaipur, Rajasthan. Salt is made here by drying its water; it supplies a large share of Rajasthan's salt. It is a Ramsar site and a winter home for flamingos. In 2019 thousands of migratory birds died here from avian botulism (a poison made by bacteria)."),
 P("Wular Lake", [74.57, 34.36], "The largest freshwater lake in India, in Bandipora, Kashmir. The Jhelum flows in and out of it, so it acts as a natural flood basin. It is a Ramsar site. The Tulbul navigation project on its outlet has been disputed by Pakistan under the Indus Waters Treaty. Dal Lake, in Srinagar city, is a different and smaller lake."),
 P("Loktak Lake", [93.79, 24.53], "The largest freshwater lake in North-East India, in Manipur. It is famous for phumdis: floating mats of soil, plants and roots. On the biggest phumdis lies Keibul Lamjao, the only floating national park in the world, the last home of the sangai (Manipur's brow-antlered deer, its state animal). Loktak is a Ramsar site but is on the Montreux Record because of the Ithai barrage and pollution.", s="Manipur"),
 P("Majuli", [94.20, 26.95], "A large river island in the Brahmaputra in Assam, once called the largest river island in the world. In 2016 it became India's first island district. It is the centre of Assamese Vaishnavism: the satras (monasteries) started in the tradition of the saint Srimanta Sankardeva (15th–16th century). It is shrinking because the river eats away its banks."),
 P("Kavaratti (Lakshadweep)", [72.64, 10.56], "The capital of Lakshadweep, India's smallest union territory: 36 coral islands in the Arabian Sea, of which about 10 are lived on. They are atolls, ring-shaped coral reefs round a lagoon. The Eight Degree Channel separates Minicoy from the Maldives, and the Nine Degree Channel separates Minicoy from the rest of Lakshadweep. Most people are Muslim and speak Malayalam (Mahl on Minicoy)."),
 P("Ten Degree Channel", [92.50, 10.00], "The sea channel along the 10° N line of latitude that separates the Andaman Islands (north) from the Nicobar Islands (south), in the Bay of Bengal. Do not mix up the channels: Ten Degree (Andaman–Nicobar), Nine Degree (Lakshadweep–Minicoy), Eight Degree (Minicoy–Maldives). The Duncan Passage separates South and Little Andaman."),
 P("Barren Island", [93.86, 12.28], "A small volcanic island in the Andaman Sea, about 135 km north-east of Port Blair. It is the only active volcano in India and in South Asia, and has erupted several times since 1991. Nobody lives on it. Narcondam, further north, is a dormant (sleeping) volcano and home of the Narcondam hornbill."),
 P("Indira Point", [93.84, 6.75], "The southern tip of Great Nicobar Island and the southernmost point of India, named after Indira Gandhi in 1985 (earlier Pygmalion Point). Its lighthouse was damaged in the 2004 tsunami. It is only about 150 km from Sumatra in Indonesia. The large Great Nicobar development project (a port, airport and township) is planned nearby."),
]),
dict(id="parks", part="India", t="National parks and wildlife", intro=(
 "A national park is land set aside by law to protect nature, where people may not live, graze or cut trees. A wildlife sanctuary has fewer rules. A tiger reserve, under Project Tiger (1973), joins a protected core with a buffer around it. A biosphere reserve, under UNESCO's Man and Biosphere programme, protects a whole region and its people. "
 "A park question asks one of three things: which animal it protects, which state it is in, or what is special about it (the first, the largest, floating, World Heritage). "
 "So group the parks by animal: the one-horned rhino in Assam, the Asiatic lion only in Gir, the tiger across many reserves, the snow leopard in Ladakh, and the cheetah, brought back from Africa, at Kuno."), P=[
 P("Jim Corbett National Park", [78.77, 29.53], "In Nainital and Pauri, Uttarakhand, on the Ramganga river. It is India's first national park, set up in 1936 as Hailey National Park, and later named after Jim Corbett, a hunter of man-eating tigers who became a conservationist and writer. Project Tiger was launched here in 1973. It is known for tigers, elephants and the gharial in the Ramganga."),
 P("Kaziranga", [93.37, 26.58], "In Golaghat and Nagaon, Assam, on the south bank of the Brahmaputra. It holds about two-thirds of the world's greater one-horned rhinos and has one of the highest densities of tigers. Its tall elephant grass floods every monsoon, and the animals move to the Karbi Anglong hills. It became a national park in 1974 and a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1985.", s="Assam"),
 P("Gir", [70.80, 21.12], "Gir National Park and Sanctuary, in Junagadh, Gujarat, is the only natural home of the Asiatic lion in the world. Their number has grown from about 20 in the early 1900s (when the Nawab of Junagadh protected them) to about 891 in the 2025 count. Because all the lions live in one area, an epidemic could wipe them out, so a second home has long been debated. The Maldhari herders live in and around Gir.", s="Gujarat", k=1),
 P("Kuno National Park", [77.33, 25.55], "In Sheopur, Madhya Pradesh. The cheetah died out in India in 1952. Under Project Cheetah, eight cheetahs from Namibia were released here on 17 September 2022, and twelve came from South Africa in 2023, the first time a large wild cat was moved between continents. Gandhi Sagar sanctuary (Madhya Pradesh) became the second cheetah home in 2025."),
 P("Sundarbans", [88.85, 21.95], "The largest mangrove forest in the world, on the delta of the Ganga, Brahmaputra and Meghna, shared by West Bengal and Bangladesh. Mangroves are trees that grow in salty tidal mud, with roots that stick up out of the water to breathe. It is named after the sundari tree. It is home to the Royal Bengal tiger, which swims between islands. It is a national park, tiger reserve, biosphere reserve, Ramsar site and World Heritage Site (1987)."),
 P("Keoladeo (Bharatpur)", [77.52, 27.17], "Keoladeo Ghana National Park, in Bharatpur, Rajasthan, is a man-made wetland, once a duck-shooting ground of the Maharajas. It is famous for winter birds; the Siberian crane used to come here but has not been seen since 2002. It is a World Heritage Site (1985) and, with Chilika, India's first Ramsar site (1981). Salim Ali, the 'Birdman of India', worked here."),
 P("Hemis National Park", [77.40, 33.90], "In Ladakh, the largest national park in India (about 4,400 sq km). It is famous for the snow leopard, which lives high in cold mountains, and is called the snow leopard capital of the world. The Hemis monastery, a Buddhist gompa with a famous festival, lies on its edge."),
 P("Great Himalayan National Park", [77.50, 31.75], "In Kullu, Himachal Pradesh, at the source of rivers such as the Tirthan and Sainj that feed the Beas. It protects high oak and conifer forests and alpine meadows of the western Himalaya, with the western tragopan (a rare pheasant, Himachal's state bird), the musk deer, the Himalayan tahr and the snow leopard. Local people's grazing rights were bought out to create it. It became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2014."),
 P("Valley of Flowers", [79.60, 30.73], "A high alpine valley in Chamoli, Uttarakhand, that bursts into flowers in the monsoon. It was made famous by the British mountaineer Frank Smythe in 1931. Together with Nanda Devi National Park it is a World Heritage Site (1988, extended 2005). The Sikh shrine of Hemkund Sahib is close by."),
 P("Silent Valley", [76.43, 11.08], "In Palakkad, Kerala, in the Nilgiri hills. It is one of the last undisturbed tropical evergreen rainforests in India. In the 1970s a plan to dam its Kunthipuzha river met the 'Save Silent Valley' movement (led by the Kerala Sasthra Sahithya Parishad and poet Sugathakumari); Indira Gandhi stopped the dam, and it became a national park in 1984. It is home to the lion-tailed macaque, an endangered monkey."),
 P("Periyar", [77.17, 9.47], "In Idukki, Kerala. A tiger reserve around a lake made by the Mullaperiyar dam (1895), which is in Kerala but owned and run by Tamil Nadu: a long-running dispute about its safety. It is known for elephants, which visitors watch from boats on the lake."),
 P("Dehing Patkai", [95.45, 27.30], "In Dibrugarh and Tinsukia, eastern Assam, in the Patkai hills. It is the largest stretch of lowland rainforest in Assam and is called the 'Jeypore rainforest' or the 'Amazon of the East'. In 2020 an online campaign, 'Save Dehing Patkai', protested a coal mining approval, and the area became a national park in June 2021. Hoolock gibbons, India's only ape, live here.", y="2021 Q92"),
 P("Bhadla Solar Park", [71.92, 27.54], "In Jodhpur district, Rajasthan, in the Thar desert, where there is lots of sun and cheap empty land. With about 2,245 MW (megawatts) of solar panels on some 5,700 hectares, it was called the world's largest solar park in 2022 lists; China's huge parks in Qinghai and Xinjiang have since grown bigger. Other big Indian parks: Pavagada (Karnataka) and the planned Khavda renewable park in the Rann of Kutch.", y="2021 Q98", k=1),
]),
dict(id="defence", part="India", t="Defence, space and training", intro=(
 "Defence questions often ask where something is based: a command, an academy, a test range, a launch pad. "
 "A command is a big regional headquarters of a service. The Navy has three: Western (Mumbai), Eastern (Visakhapatnam) and Southern (Kochi, the training command). The Army has seven and the Air Force seven; the Andaman and Nicobar Command is the only one with all three services under one head. "
 "Missiles are tested off the Odisha coast. Satellites are launched from Sriharikota because a rocket fired east over the sea gets a push from the earth's spin, and if it fails it falls into empty water."), P=[
 P("Port Blair (Sri Vijaya Puram)", [92.73, 11.62], "The capital of the Andaman and Nicobar Islands, renamed Sri Vijaya Puram in September 2024. It is the base of the Andaman and Nicobar Command (2001), India's first and only tri-service theatre command, where the Army, Navy and Air Force work under one commander. It watches the approach to the Strait of Malacca. The Cellular Jail (Kala Pani), where the British kept freedom fighters such as V. D. Savarkar, is here. INS Utkrosh is its naval air station.", y="2021 Q85"),
 P("Sriharikota", [80.23, 13.72], "A barrier island in Tirupati district, Andhra Pradesh, between Pulicat Lake and the Bay of Bengal. ISRO's Satish Dhawan Space Centre (SHAR) is here, named after a former ISRO chief. Its east coast location near the equator helps rockets gain speed from the earth's spin, and failed rockets fall into the sea. Chandrayaan-3 (2023) and Aditya-L1 (2023) were launched from here. A second spaceport for small rockets is being built at Kulasekarapattinam, Tamil Nadu."),
 P("Thumba", [76.87, 8.53], "A fishing village near Thiruvananthapuram, Kerala. India's space programme began here when a small American Nike-Apache rocket was launched on 21 November 1963 from the Thumba Equatorial Rocket Launching Station (TERLS). It was chosen because the earth's magnetic equator passes nearby. The parts were carried by bicycle and the office was in a church. The Vikram Sarabhai Space Centre (VSSC), which designs ISRO's rockets, is here."),
 P("Abdul Kalam Island", [87.08, 20.76], "A small island off Bhadrak, Odisha, formerly Wheeler Island, renamed in 2015 after Dr A. P. J. Abdul Kalam, the 'Missile Man of India' and later President. It is DRDO's main site for testing long-range missiles such as Agni and the K-series. Chandipur, near Balasore on the coast, is the other DRDO test range (the Integrated Test Range), used for Prithvi, Akash and other missiles."),
 P("Pokhran", [71.92, 26.92], "A town in Jaisalmer, Rajasthan, where India tested nuclear devices: 'Smiling Buddha' on 18 May 1974 (under Indira Gandhi) and Operation Shakti, five tests on 11 and 13 May 1998 (under Atal Bihari Vajpayee), after which India declared itself a nuclear weapon state. 11 May is celebrated as National Technology Day. The Pokhran field firing range is also used for army exercises."),
 P("Mumbai", [72.83, 18.93], "Headquarters of the Western Naval Command, the sword arm of the Navy, facing the Arabian Sea and Pakistan. The Mazagon Dock in Mumbai builds India's submarines (the Kalvari class) and destroyers. The Gateway of India and the naval dockyard stand side by side."),
 P("Visakhapatnam", [83.28, 17.69], "Headquarters of the Eastern Naval Command, facing the Bay of Bengal. India's nuclear-powered ballistic-missile submarines (INS Arihant, INS Arighaat) are based on this coast; a secret base for them is being built at Rambilli nearby. The city hosted the International Fleet Review and exercise MILAN in 2026. The Hindustan Shipyard is here.", k=1),
 P("Kochi", [76.27, 9.96], "Headquarters of the Southern Naval Command, the training command of the Navy. INS Vikrant, India's first home-built aircraft carrier, was built at Cochin Shipyard and joined the Navy in September 2022; the ship's name comes from the first Vikrant of 1961. Kochi was also the first European settlement in India (the Portuguese, 1500s)."),
 P("Karwar (INS Kadamba)", [74.12, 14.80], "In Uttara Kannada, Karnataka. INS Kadamba is a large naval base built under Project Seabird, meant to become the largest naval base east of the Suez Canal. Ships can leave from here without passing busy Mumbai harbour. The Kali river meets the sea here."),
 P("Khadakwasla (NDA)", [73.77, 18.44], "Near Pune, Maharashtra. The National Defence Academy (1954) trains cadets of the Army, Navy and Air Force together for three years before they go to their own academies. It was the first joint-services academy in the world. Women cadets were admitted from 2022 after a Supreme Court order."),
 P("Dehradun (IMA)", [78.00, 30.33], "The Indian Military Academy (1932) trains army officers; its passing-out parade is held at the Chetwode Hall. The Rashtriya Indian Military College (RIMC) and the Forest Research Institute are also in Dehradun. Other academies: Air Force Academy at Dundigal (Hyderabad), Officers Training Academy at Chennai and Gaya."),
 P("Ezhimala", [75.20, 12.03], "In Kannur, Kerala. The Indian Naval Academy moved here from Goa and was opened in 2009; it is the largest naval academy in Asia. Cadets from the NDA and direct-entry officers of the Navy and Coast Guard train here, and it gives a B.Tech degree. It sits on the Ezhimala headland, a hill jutting into the Arabian Sea that was once the seat of the ancient Mushika kings."),
 P("Gurugram (ISA and INDU)", [76.95, 28.40], "Gurugram, Haryana, holds two answers. The International Solar Alliance (ISA), started by India and France at the Paris climate summit in 2015, has its headquarters here; it is the first international body with its headquarters in India and aims to bring cheap solar power to sunny countries ('One Sun, One World, One Grid'). The Indian National Defence University (INDU), a university for defence studies, is being built at Binola nearby.", y="2021 Q52, 2021 Q59, 2021 Q81", s="Haryana"),
 P("Bengaluru (HAL)", [77.66, 12.96], "Hindustan Aeronautics Limited (HAL), the government's aircraft maker, builds the Tejas light combat aircraft, the Dhruv helicopter (ALH) and the Prachand light combat helicopter here, and makes the Su-30MKI under licence at Nashik. Bengaluru also has ISRO's headquarters and the Aeronautical Development Agency, so it is called the aerospace capital of India. The Aero India air show is held at Yelahanka air base."),
]),
]
